lling-based picture.", "watch": "Any further movement in the polls after the audio controversy, or signs Osborn is regaining traction with rural voters, could tighten the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Pete Ricketts (R) is running.", "Dan Osborn is an independent standing in for the Democratic side."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's Senate race pits appointed incumbent Pete Ricketts, a wealthy former governor seeking his first full term, against independent Dan Osborn, who is running a populist campaign and appears on the Democratic side of the ballot. The state's Republican lean gives Ricketts a strong structural edge, and he has out-raised Osborn roughly two to one while holding a large cash advantage.\n\nOsborn kept the race competitive for months, with polls showing a narrow contest, but leaked recordings of him disparaging some Trump voters in mid-September handed Republicans an attack line, and a GOP super PAC entered with a $1.4 million ad buy. Prediction markets now give Ricketts roughly a two-in-three chance, and most expert raters call the race Likely Republican, though Osborn's independent appeal keeps some uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1905, "margin": -4.44}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1701, "margin": -6.0}]}